 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/from-massachusetts/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is South Carolina cheaper than Massachusetts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On housing, dramatically: a typical home costs close to half. The income tax rates are nearer than people expect, 5 percent flat there against 5.21 percent top here. South Carolina's deduction of up to $30,000 for a couple, the property tax at less than half the bill, the smaller car excise and no estate tax all move the total the same direction."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"If I move to South Carolina but keep a house in Massachusetts, does the Massachusetts estate tax still apply?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"To the house, yes. Massachusetts taxes real estate and tangible property located in Massachusetts even when the owner dies domiciled elsewhere, and the $2 million filing trigger counts your total worldwide estate. Your investment and retirement accounts, once you are domiciled in South Carolina, are outside the Massachusetts estate tax. Selling the old house completes the exit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the Massachusetts estate tax and does South Carolina have one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Massachusetts taxes estates above $2 million, one of the lowest thresholds in the country, with a $99,600 credit for deaths on or after January 1, 2023. A paid-off house plus retirement accounts crosses $2 million without anyone feeling rich. South Carolina has no estate tax and no inheritance tax; where you are domiciled at death decides which rules apply."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Massachusetts or South Carolina tax Social Security?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Neither taxes Social Security. The difference is everything after it. Massachusetts taxes private pensions, 401(k) and IRA withdrawals at its flat rate with no senior exclusion. South Carolina deducts up to $15,000 of income per person at 65 and older, with the retirement income deduction of up to $10,000 counting against that same $15,000. Military retirement is fully exempt in South Carolina."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I have a Massachusetts government pension. Should I know something first?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it runs against the move: Massachusetts does not tax its own contributory public pensions, but South Carolina taxes them like any pension past its deductions. For a retired Massachusetts teacher or state worker, that single pension line can offset the other savings. Run both states' math on your actual pension before you list the house."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens to my car excise tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It gets smaller for most movers. Massachusetts charges a fixed $25 per $1,000 of valuation, 2.5 percent, everywhere in the state. Horry County taxes vehicles on value too, at an effective bite closer to half that: about $362 a year on a $30,000 car in the unincorporated county, plus a $50 road fee and a one-time $250 fee at first registration."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does a house cost in Myrtle Beach compared with Massachusetts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A typical home here costs about $342,000 against a Massachusetts statewide average of about $669,000 on the same July 2026 Zillow measure, close to double. Greater Boston averages more than that. See how much further every dollar goes here compared with your hometown on our cost of living calculator."}}]}</script>
